consolidate authors and works before editions?
identity mappings, then decisions
every catalog table needs a "redirect"
if we later find out that it's identical to someone else, we set the redirect
(and make sure there's no redirect to a redirect. only singe-hop redirects.)

a column on the identity mapping that says "flag for review"?

do we have to hold the whole thing in memory at the same time?
no
and thank god, because openlibrary is ~90Gb
do we have to hold all of one type?
maybe
we do have to hold the identifiers, and any field that can be used for that (name)
we don't have to hold all the extra fields
basically there are three types of fields in this case:
- hard identifiers (isbn)
- soft identifiers (name, birthyear, title, publishedyear)
- decorators (everything else)

there's a difference between bootstrapping the database from nothing and updates?
maybe not?
if we can process one at a time, then it's just "look for a matching. attach or create"

the difference between identity and decisions:
identity finds or create catalog entries based on fundamental identifiers
decision is filling in the non-fundamental identifiers, or improving the existing ones
(which shouldn't break any existing mapping, like turning "A. Smith" into "Adam Smith")

# authors:

when matching names, need to be unicode aware, and standardize the strings
we will be combining data from english sources and non-english sources
there is a world where each author has a list of possible names, and we can match on any of them
which would capture "correct name" (Anne Brontë) and "anglicized name" (Anne Bronte)

there is two problems we're solving here:
- type 1: combining multiple authors into one. easier
- type 2: splitting one author into many. much much much more difficult

## type 1

two authors probably match if their last name and birthyear are the same
if that's true, then they match if the first name loosely matches
loosely matches means
- R and Robert
- Bob and Robert

anther way to tell if authors are the same:
- if they look different in some ways
- and they both point to different books
- but we've determined that those books are the same
- but but if their names are very different, maybe we flag for review

we want some way to visually see all these connections, to see all the books that are considered "the same" and all the authors on them that are considered "the same"

## type 2

openlibrary has some problems with combining too many people into a single author
e.g. Butler, /authors/OL2699407A
it's just a last name, and is the listed author for a ton of books
and openlibrary calls them all the same person

rules:
if there's just a name
if there's no other information
split it

what do we do when we identify a type 2?
flag it for review?
split it into one author for every work it's attached to?
keep it as one "author" but identify it to the user as a "probably bad data, very sorry"

# works

i'd just keep them the same

decisions:
work can take their title from the most common title of all the editions
that might be better, because openlibrary work titles are iffy
(see battle cry for freedom)

# editions/books

modern books
two editions are the same if they have the same isbn
so a book is a thing with a set of isbns
any processed record with one of those isbns is part of that edition

we create an identity mapping from the processed edition to the catalog edition
if the catalog edition doesn't exist for that set of isbn, we create it

do we need a table of isbn mappings? or generic identity mappings?
or can we take advantage of postgres to do a better job of handling lists
so like each catalog edition has a list of isbns for it

older (pre-isbn) books:
if some identifier exists, use that
it's the same if it has an identical title & publishedyear?
if the same if it has the same title and the the underlying authors have been tagged as the same?

also, what do we do with books that are jsut one specific book
/books/OL7079371M

# process

fill in ISBN table from all found ISBNs in all editions
